#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446093} :activity/id #uuid "02067a53-4847-5e9e-8b0d-b2b3da7d2999" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b0966e0-07b0-5f6b-880a-9d5d92900e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446082} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8e26dd-8c1f-5a51-bd64-4a8c0ed95c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446083} {:answer/id #uuid "72f83e85-8dd3-534d-b4ca-baaac2319fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446084} {:answer/id #uuid "a8406b13-840b-5d77-8dd2-2d014e10235d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446085} {:answer/id #uuid "abf0e08f-d55a-5c7e-8ead-34398d2d59d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446086}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446084} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a6a40a-0b17-512b-bf03-9bbe6e406509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fd6674b6-1720-503b-8769-c73b368922ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-72188" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region that lies outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and inside the square defined by the lines $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=-1$ and $y=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/e7/e762e510b690d65dfc8365d041c734d7b16611411004723a2abdbb74bfb41cc3.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/b7/b72bb25ce1dcce3dc7792bbbcb8a878dde14df3a90c9243a3c75ac26a19aaa47.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/eb/eb34633d98011ed7050694c722bc7c736a95939783efec3321d95517695642e2.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "24b1c7cb-ecec-5845-8416-fdfafa79a79d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17be251d-7119-505a-822a-7b4f4a540bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336254} {:answer/id #uuid "28248254-c521-555f-a8eb-3698fd411628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336409} {:answer/id #uuid "54358dc3-968d-51c2-b7a6-0a9e65676557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336809} {:answer/id #uuid "559f9ad0-2178-5631-a30c-df72edf7e3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336818} {:answer/id #uuid "55dfee5e-aee0-54ba-a2ef-8bb2f5c930f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336254} :answer-field/id #uuid "acf0a001-ed03-592c-9465-79a8c55ab0a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bcd8034-22f3-5298-b8d3-b69cdcbc84fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72185" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region inside the circumference ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/09/0960063edcce8ccd55dc2f77147c4a4982645c180e8b998d7e520c65131ea045.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "42ed5761-ab81-502e-952a-3422383e386b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e353de59-45ed-5d3e-a3c7-c62ccb8bfee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/32/32e6ed07f3229c8e6016d12dd35c42597888662fbc909f53bc07e495a73958e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389596} {:answer/id #uuid "189f5878-608c-523c-8af3-61b336e7fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389597} {:answer/id #uuid "cae29720-9bdf-5675-b7dc-87fb52dc9fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/36/36275eef6e634c83c614626bc2af6b97cd5bafddc8c02b64ba2a1aa7ea983317.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389598} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e89d64-16cb-5f6e-924f-288db18c0bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/de/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389599} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e82089-b886-5a96-8c4e-a2c7d256121e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5ac45c6757e187796674ca4beda4ff73c60835b401df302bfd8a91fa5c98b68c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389599} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e761f31-f26b-54c2-90ef-d2fbb7506ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2da77bc4-04e1-56ef-a7c8-2c62ea7852fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-168140" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/1b/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)"} :step/id #uuid "6b389470-3267-59d1-924e-08b2f27327ab"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cebe51cf-7c64-56b5-90cf-d131aa350434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403566} {:answer/id #uuid "bb98efc4-1808-5a18-907a-2ed362b46813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403567} {:answer/id #uuid "715194f5-7e2b-5553-92e1-418bb1db22f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403568} {:answer/id #uuid "52989cfd-9a0b-5436-a602-9bec29db010b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403569} {:answer/id #uuid "23c11e6d-f7bd-5794-99cd-309344d7fab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403569} :answer-field/id #uuid "1886eec5-845d-5de6-8b01-c5885246a233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d94b1132-4667-52b0-83fe-bd8235741b13" :question/math-academy-id "q-72345" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the line $y=x$ and the curve $y={x}^{3},$ in the first quadrant?\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/1d/1d1c1cbe30e6f612f824adc76d712a98194ffccc5edefc11cccdaf07204513ba.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "79b60224-d789-538c-8707-f34af9a3d64e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393194 